## What I built

**Server-only IPython kernel manager** that owns one real, interruptible kernel per lesson session — so Code/Sandbox widgets can run real PyTorch/TF/OpenCV instead of Pyodide.

**`scripts/kernel/kernel_bridge.py`** — a long-lived Python process that owns one kernel via `jupyter_client.KernelManager`, speaking line-delimited JSON over stdio. Key design choices:
- Forces the kernel onto the bridge's own interpreter (`km._kernel_spec = KernelSpec(argv=[sys.executable, -m, ipykernel_launcher, …])`), so kernel-venv == US-196 runtime venv with **no dependency on a registered kernelspec**.
- A dedicated stdin reader thread lets `interrupt` (`km.interrupt_kernel()` = SIGINT) land *while* an execute is blocking on iopub; execute/restart/shutdown run serialized on the main thread.

**`src/lib/server/kernelManager.ts`** — `KernelSessionManager` (+ singleton `kernelManager`), same spawn/`detached:true`/process-group-kill discipline as `stt.ts`/`generation.ts`. Covers every AC:
- One kernel per (courseSlug, lessonSlug), reused; concurrent first-execs deduped via a `creating` map.
- Idle reaping after TTL; per-session execution serialization via a promise-chain mutex (interrupt bypasses it).
- execute / interrupt / restart / shutdown; **force-kill of the process group on execution timeout** (`process.kill(-pid, 'SIGKILL')`, gated by `instanceof RealChildProcess`).
- Missing US-196 runtime → `KernelRuntimeNotInstalledError`.

**`src/lib/server/kernelManager.test.ts`** — 5 fast unit tests (scripted fake bridge) + **6 real-kernel integration tests** verifying the execute / interrupt / restart / reap / timeout cycle against an actual IPython kernel (auto-discovers a python with jupyter_client+ipykernel; `it.skip`s when none).

## Quality
- Typecheck clean, eslint clean on new files.
- `kernelManager.test.ts`: 11/11 pass, both standalone and inside the full suite.
- Full suite: 1141 passed; the only failing files (`extend/page.test.tsx`, `extendApply.test.ts`) are pre-existing full-suite-parallelism flakes — both pass in isolation and neither is touched by this story (verified by moving my files aside).
- `prd.json` US-197 `passes=true`, committed in HEAD (`6bf509b`).

The one mid-work bug worth flagging: the interrupt integration test initially flaked under full-suite CPU load (fixed pre-interrupt sleep firing before slow kernel startup) — fixed with a warm-up execute so the interrupt is always timed against a running cell.
